i'm here talking this afternoon to  benoit felton benoit you're going to be  talking here at the ftth council about  some market research you just completed  in portugal and france but previously  you did some in sweden before we start  talking about the new stuff just  reminders of the findings you had from  that report in sweden so what we were  trying to achieve  was to basically measure attitudes  and satisfaction  for broadband users  and then look at whether  fiber made a difference in these  attitudes and  satisfaction what we saw in sweden  essentially is that first of all fibre  users are very satisfied with their  broadband much more so than dsl users  but also that dsl users like fibre users  perceive fiber as a better platform  and finally that there is a significant  difference in behavior  both the time spent online and the type  of things that are done online are  different and more advanced for fiber  users  okay and what about uh what about this  new research so what we wanted to  achieve with the france and portugal  surveys was put this in perspective of  market maturity sweden is a very mature  market fiber has been around for a long  time  portugal is a relatively mature market  but it's not been around for that long  and france is a fairly new market to  fiber  and  what we saw  was that typically  the perception of fiber by dsl users  is not as good in portugal as it is in  sweden and it's even worse in france why  because fiber hasn't been around for a  long time and therefore dsl users have  never heard of people who have fiber or  experience fiber so they think it's just  as good as what they've currently got or  just as bad depending on how you want to  frame it  and  so essentially we see that maturity does  have a significant impact the other  thing which was quite interesting  is that  the behavior of dsl and fiber users in  france and portugal is not that  different  our assumption is that  the time that they've had the new  platform is not long enough for them to  have explored its possibilities whereas  in sweden they've had fiber on average  for three or four years and so they have  explored those possibilities and they  use it more and for more advanced things  so going forward is it all about fiber  well  quite clearly what this analysis  demonstrates is that fiber does make a  difference for end users but it also  highlights the key challenge for  operators deploying fiber which is  how do you convince  someone who's never used it that the  experience is different and radically  better and  what we see is that part of it is market  maturity in other words the longer fiber  has been available on the market the  more people are aware that it is a  better platform  of course you can accelerate that  through marketing campaigns and finding  clever ways to get people to experience  it without necessarily owning it but  there is an element of this which will  be solved by time  okay  just before you go one thing i'm really  interested in is the sheer scale of the  work that is involved in producing these  surveys i mean how do you go about  finding this data  well the the it is challenging and and  it's  uh it's even more challenging because  we're doing this in numerous countries  uh but i have to say that the internet  has been a ruin to market researchers  because what we used to have to do over  the phone  and took a lot of time and manpower to  do we can now do over the internet where  basically  people will fill in surveys  and we will then analyze those forms  it's still a pretty big task but  actually the biggest challenge is not so  much in  gathering the data the biggest challenge  is in building  a questionnaire that will not skew the  results  and also that will deliver  the insights that you want rather than  something that you're not sure you can  use a lot of market studies  unfortunately suffer from poor wording  and one of the things that we did to  ensure that this didn't happen was we  hired an expert in  questionnaire buildup  to make sure that we weren't skewing the  data in any way  and i'm very proud of the results that  we've got and i invite everyone to uh  have a look at them either here at the  conference or through the series of  webinars that we're going to do  afterwards with the council  benoit thanks very much for talking to  us you're welcome  so some interesting new research soon to  be available on portugal and france
 

